.DS_Store
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# setuptools editable-install metadata
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bulk re-downloadable caches only. Anything the engine's published numbers depend on
# must stay tracked, however small -- a clean checkout has to reproduce the manuscripts,
# and silently falling back to an IPCC default when a cache is absent is not reproducible.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SPATIAL INPUTS. The sector layout replaced the old "ignore data/gis/* and whitelist the
# dozen files we actually version" arrangement with its inverse: the spatial assets live
# under data/sectors/<sector>/gis/ and data/shared/gis/ and are TRACKED, because a clean
# checkout has to reproduce the manuscripts and the grid cube has to be offline-
# deterministic. Only the two bulk, re-fetchable items are excluded, by name.
#
# This inversion is the whole reason these three lines exist. Under the old block these
# five files were ignored by falling outside a whitelist; relocating them silently made
# them trackable, and the next `git add` would have put 41 MB of re-fetchable geometry
# into plain git with no LFS rule - including the 28 MB COD-AB archive. The exclusion is
# re-expressed here deliberately rather than left to be rediscovered.
#
# The COD-AB administrative bundle (28 MB archive + 9.7 MB extracted admin2 geometry, plus
# its sidecar): a humanitarian-data download, re-fetchable, and not read by any published
# number - PAK_ADM1/ADM2 are the versioned controls the manuscript spatial joins use.
/data/shared/gis/cod_ab/
# The exact-area ADM2 x grid partition (3.6 MB), re-derived by the GIS extra from the
# versioned boundaries. scripts/raw_data_manifest.py skips it for this reason and
# pkinv.report.data_provenance.universe() names it; keep those three in step.
/data/shared/gis/adm2_intersections_267x340.csv
/data/shared/gis/adm2_intersections_267x340.csv.txt
data/faostat_cache/
# Permissioned aviation observations.  The extraction query and aggregate coverage
# products are published; OpenSky flight/state-vector rows are not redistributed.
data/private/aviation/
# data/cache/aviation/ is TRACKED now - see .gitattributes. Nothing raw is excluded.
# ...except the licence that governs the ADS-B payloads. The bytes stay out of git because
# ODbL is share-alike and this repository's data licence is CC-BY-4.0; the LICENCE saying so
# must be tracked, or the carve-out exists only in a commit message.
!data/cache/aviation/adsblol/LICENSE-ODbL

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ORIGINAL SOURCES ARE TRACKED. All of them, whatever they weigh.
#
# This block used to exclude 3.2 GB of government scans and survey archives, on the reasoning
# that they "must be backed up separately (external drive / cloud)". There is no cloud copy
# and there never was, so the exclusion was not deferring the storage problem - it was
# guaranteeing that the only copy of documents that are in several cases no longer published
# lived outside version control, unhashed and unnoticed.
#
# Large files now go to Git LFS by type; see .gitattributes for the rules and for why the
# split is by file type rather than by a list of names. data/raw_manifest.csv records every
# (since rc36, INCLUDING data/raw/ - it had claimed that coverage and held none of it)
# raw file with its size, hash, origin and licence, scripts/verify_raw_data.py fails if any
# is missing or altered, and scripts/mirror_raw_data.sh copies exactly those paths to a
# second location - because tracking a file in git puts a second copy on the SAME disk and
# is not a backup.
#
# The one raw tree deliberately outside this repository is data/cache/aviation/adsblol/:
# 9.1 GB of ADS-B under ODbL, kept separate so that share-alike data is not relicensed by
# inclusion into a CC-BY-4.0 core. It is hashed and verified like everything else.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (nothing under sources/ is excluded)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LaTeX build artefacts (regenerable; .tex/.bib/.bbl/.pdf are kept)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each paper compiles to paper.pdf beside its source; `make sector-papers` then copies it
# to papers/pdf/<sector>_paper.pdf, which is the tracked artefact. Committing both would
# version the same document twice under two names.
papers/*/paper.pdf
papers/*/supplement.pdf
# rc36: three PDFs committed under other names slipped the two rules above and
# went three weeks stale beside the papers/pdf/ copies. Any PDF under a paper's
# own directory is a build artefact; only papers/pdf/ is tracked.
papers/*/*.pdf
!papers/pdf/*.pdf
# The national supplement compiles beside its source and `make paper-national` copies it to
# papers/pdf/national_supplement.pdf, which is the tracked artefact. paper_national.pdf itself
# is tracked for historical reasons and is left alone; this stops the SECOND copy appearing.
paper_national/versions/*/supplement.pdf
# A zip of papers/pdf/, made by hand to send somewhere. Every file inside it is already
# versioned, and an 8 MB archive of tracked files is a second vintage waiting to be quoted.
papers/pdf.zip
# Same reasoning, same shape: hand-made bundles of a sector's raw documents and parsed
# activity files, for sending to someone who does not have the repository. Every file
# inside is tracked, so the archive is a second vintage the moment it is quoted from.
/agriculture.zip
/*_bundle.zip
/*_audit.zip
/agriculture_*.zip
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.blg
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk
*.synctex.gz
*.spl
*.toc
*.lof
*.lot
.venv/

# The rail, kiln, landfill and road networks, and the FIRMS extraction, were whitelisted
# back into the retired data/gis/* block. They are versioned by default now, under
# data/sectors/{railways,industry,waste,transport}/gis/ and
# data/sectors/agriculture/gis/manual/ (sources/OSM archives stay ignored).
# The OSM extract, the ELK bundle, the UNFCCC national communication and the HIES microdata
# payloads were all excluded here on size, each with a re-fetch URL beside it. They are
# tracked now, for the reason at the top of this file: a re-fetch URL is a promise about
# somebody else's server, and the HIES microdata in particular is a survey release that is
# withdrawn and replaced rather than archived. The one file over GitHub's limit among them
# goes to LFS by name; see .gitattributes.
# rc39 A12: the assembled deposit capsule, 148 MB of copied tree. Built by
# scripts/build_capsule.py on demand and never committed - it is a COPY of files
# already tracked, so committing it would double the repository to no purpose.
/dist/

# run ledgers and reproduction logs (rc41); never products
/logs/

# Commit-message drafts and working notes: passed to scripts/gate_detached.sh by path,
# never part of the release tree.
scratch/

# rc44: two more assembled copies, ignored for the reason /dist/ is. derived/ is a
# byte-identical mirror of outputs/ (706 files), and pakemis_verification_external/ is
# per-sector bundles of manifest + source PDFs, 7.9 GB of documents already tracked under
# sources/. Both are built on demand; committing either would duplicate the repository to
# no purpose, and the noise gate correctly refused a commit that would have swept them in.
/derived/
/pakemis_verification_external/
# Obsolete layout only. Sector/shared canonical CSVs are versioned reviewer artifacts.
/data/canonical/

# LOCAL WORKING FILES at the repository root, untracked on purpose. The two workbooks are
# byte-identical duplicates of documents already held under sources/Power Plants/ (the
# copies the ingest scripts read); the Methods assets are design work from the /methods
# round that lives in the Explorer repository. Nothing here is an input to a published
# number. They are listed because scripts/release_fixpoint.py refuses to cut a manifest
# from a tree carrying uncommitted CONTENT, and an untracked stray reads as content.
/PakEmis_Methods_SVGs/
/PakEmis_Methods_SVGs.zip
/methods_frontend.png
/power_plants_data_preparation.xlsx
/power_plants_emissions.xlsx
# A 13.2 GB hand-made archive of the whole data/ tree (6,534 files, 23 Sep 2026). Git already
# versions that tree, so committing it would store every byte twice and blow past GitHub's
# limits; left untracked it reads as uncommitted CONTENT and blocks the release fixpoint.
/data.zip
# A handoff prompt for a timed autonomous run, placed at the root because its own launch
# instructions require that location. Local campaign scaffolding, not project documentation.
/PakEmis_24h_Claude_Master_Prompt.md
/PakEmis_24_hour_prompt.txt

# Other authors' figures, kept locally for the design study only (see figure_design_study.md)
/paper_national/figures/redesign/reference_board/
